    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so i keep hearing a lot about how cities need to change how they do zoning to fix the housing crisis. like i see people talking about high density stuff being the only way forward. can u explain what the main arguments are for and against changing zoning laws to allow more apartments in neighborhoods that are mostly single family homes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Zoning for higher density housing, like apartments, in single-family neighborhoods aims to increase affordable housing and urban density. Proponents argue it's needed to address shortages, make efficient use of land, and reduce sprawl. Some also believe it can boost local economies and create vibrant, diverse communities.\n\nOpponents of changes argue it compromises the character and quality of single-family neighborhoods. They're concerned about potential overcrowding, strain on public services, and gentrification that could displace existing residents. Both sides highlight the need for balanced growth that respects community goals and sustainability.",
